    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1608">

                <pb facs="1175" n="1059" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg1059"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.51/111.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                            French Ambassador</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-02-11">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 11, 1915</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Ambassador</hi>: Referring to the
                    Department’s note No. 1449, of February 4, 1915, relative to a report that the
                    Carrancista forces at Vera Cruz have seized 500 rifles and a certain quantity of
                    cartridges which were stored in the custom house at Vera Cruz, belonging to the
                    French Government, I beg to inform you that the Department is in receipt of a
                    telegram from the American Consul at Vera Cruz, dated February 8, stating that
                    these munitions of war <pb facs="1176" n="1060" xml:id="pg1060"/> have been
                    confiscated, notwithstanding a promise made to the French Consul that the return
                    of the shipment to France would be permitted.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. J. Bryan.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
